Gateway selector receive: TinyTuya 1.20.0 causal reproduction and minimal fix

Scope

Only GatewayBroker's selector-driven parent.receive() temporarily disables
parent.retry. A finally block restores the exact previous value before dispatch,
disconnect handling, another queued operation, heartbeat, or worker cleanup.
Standalone devices, child status/control retry policy, socket timeout,
socketRetryLimit, socketRetryDelay, heartbeat/select intervals, poll cadence,
locks and broker reconnect/backoff logic are unchanged.

This is a targeted empty-frame retry fix, not a nonblocking receive API or a
general upper bound on gateway latency. No live deployment is part of this work.

Dependency control flow (installed TinyTuya 1.20.0)

core/XenonDevice.py:
  receive() delegates directly to self._send_receive(None).
  _send_receive() sets max_recv_retries to 0 if retry is false, otherwise to
  socketRetryLimit. It starts recv_retries at 0 and calls _receive().
  An empty TuyaMessage is an acknowledged frame: partial_success becomes true,
  payload becomes None, and the empty-payload branch increments recv_retries.
  With retry=True and socketRetryLimit=1, 1 is not greater than 1, so the loop
  calls _receive() a second time. If that raises socket.timeout, the receive-only
  path returns None without closing a persistent socket.
  With retry=False, max_recv_retries=0; the first increment makes 1 > 0, ending
  the loop. _process_message() returns None for the empty frame. No second
  _receive() is attempted.

core/Device.py:
  heartbeat(nowait=True) uses _send_receive(..., getresponse=False). It sends the
  heartbeat but leaves its reply for the selector receive path.

Single-owner reasoning

The owner thread drains broker calls, checks the connection, sends a due
heartbeat, drains cached pushes, and selects/receives. Calls submitted while
heartbeat/receive is running remain queued until the next drain. There is no
await, new lock, worker wakeup, or callback invocation inside the temporary
retry=False region. All supported status, control, updatedps and configure
operations execute on that same worker after restoration. No global retry
setting is changed. External code directly using parent transport would violate
the existing ownership contract and is not made safe by this change.

An asyncio caller cancellation does not interrupt the Python I/O thread.
Shutdown joins the owner before disposing of it; cancellation of the stop waiter
does not cancel that join. If a first-frame read is blocked, restoration happens
when the read exits, not at the instant shutdown is requested. A finally block
also restores the value if a BaseException unwinds the receive call.

Deterministic tests, with no device network

tests/test_gateway_receive.py uses the installed Device/XenonDevice methods.
The direct reproduction substitutes only _receive() results: an empty
TuyaMessage followed by socket.timeout. A spy proves receive() invokes the real
_send_receive(None), with two reads for retry=True and one for retry=False;
both return None. A version guard requires this reviewed TinyTuya 1.20.0.

The broker reproduction goes further: the real heartbeat, receive,
_send_receive, _receive, _recv_all, decryptor and frame decoder run against a
scripted byte socket. Sending the heartbeat makes a complete empty reply
readable and enqueues a call. An empty socket models a five-second timeout by
advancing a private clock, never sleeping or using a virtual asyncio loop.
The baseline comparison leaves retry enabled by making the candidate's setter
a no-op; it does not reimplement TinyTuya's control flow. The candidate uses
the real setter and the same broker and input bytes.

  baseline: 2 _receive calls, 2 socket reads, queued work after 5010 modeled ms
  candidate: 1 _receive call, 1 socket read, queued work after 10 modeled ms

The remaining 10 ms is TinyTuya's existing heartbeat sendWait, included in both
cases. These deterministic times demonstrate control flow, not a hardware SLA.
The test also checks that heartbeat/connect/disconnect behavior and all listed
transport timing settings are unchanged for the complete empty-ACK case.

Further tests exercise a real worker thread with queued real child status,
control, updatedps and GatewayConnection.configure operations. None executes
during receive; all observe the original retry setting. Child status still
reads past an empty ACK to obtain its response. Coalesced ACK/data frames for
protocols 3.3, 3.4 and 3.5 leave the data for a later selector iteration, with
queued work serviced in between. Tests cover restoration after data, None,
exception, BaseException cancellation, stop and cancelled stop waiters.

Malformed and truncated frames: explicit limits and changed behavior

  Complete frame in several available TCP chunks:
    TinyTuya's first _receive/_recv_all still assembles and decodes it under
    either retry setting. TCP reads and protocol frames are not interchangeable.

  Truncated header/body with no remaining bytes:
    The FIRST _receive can still block until the existing socket timeout.
    TinyTuya returns None, preserves the persistent socket, and does not retain
    _recv_all's partial bytes across calls. This pre-existing loss of partial
    framing state is not repaired by retry suppression. A selector only proves
    some bytes are readable, not that an entire frame is available.

  DecodeError (e.g. oversized header or failed 3.5 GCM authentication):
    retry=True can read a subsequent valid frame on the same socket. retry=False
    exhausts the read-retry budget immediately, returns ERR_PAYLOAD (904), and
    TinyTuya force-closes the socket if there was no valid frame. Tests explicitly
    compare both behaviors and verify the corrupt frame is never dispatched as
    valid data. This changes recovery timing and can discard a following good
    frame on that connection. The broker then uses its existing reconnect path,
    after retry has been restored. Reconnect code/settings are unchanged; it
    would be incorrect to claim identical reconnect frequency on corrupt input.

  Complete decrypted payload that is invalid JSON:
    TinyTuya returns ERR_JSON (900) under both settings, with one _receive call;
    it does not force-close this persistent socket merely for invalid JSON.

Other blocking paths remain: prefix resynchronization inside a first frame,
TinyTuya's separate socket-error reconnect/send retries, and synchronous child
requests. parent.retry only controls the additional receive/DecodeError budget;
it does not disable every retry mechanism in TinyTuya.

Monitor comparison

TinyTuya 1.20.0 core/Monitor.py intentionally uses its own framing path.
_handle_readable() makes one sock.recv(4096), appends to state.recv_buffer, and
calls _process_buffer(). Incomplete prefixes, headers and bodies remain buffered
until another readable event. Complete empty payloads are discarded without
waiting for a following frame. Tests run those real Monitor methods on an empty
ACK and a fragmented data frame without constructing its sockets/reactor.

Monitor keeps sockets blocking for compatibility with TinyTuya's send path; it
avoids the particular read-until-complete loop by buffering across select events.
It also forces socketRetryLimit=0 during reactor ownership and has a separate
reconnect workflow. Those broader lifecycle/send changes are not copied here.

Recommendation

Use the local retry suppression as a narrowly scoped candidate for review and a
future authorized canary. It removes the reproduced second-frame wait while
preserving ownership and ordinary command retry behavior. Accepting it includes
the documented earlier close/reconnect on DecodeError; strict equivalence for
malformed-frame recovery is not claimed.

No evidence here requires a large new parser to fix the complete empty-ACK
stall. A buffered receive helper WOULD be needed to guarantee prompt service
under fragmented/truncated input or retain partial frames across timeouts. That
is a separate design problem requiring coordination with synchronous child
requests, encryption/session state, CID caches, and socket replacement. A helper
that merely calls _receive() once would still block on incomplete first frames.
